<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Appendix: Proofs</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Definitions</h3>

<div id="A1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition A.1</span></span><span id="A1.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A position-wise feedforward network (FFN) with input/output width <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and hidden width <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{ff}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>ff</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{ff}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the function</p>
<table id="A1.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{FF}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}^{d},\;x\mapsto W^{(2)}\sigma(W^{(1)}x+b^{(1)})+b^{(2)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>FF</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{FF}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}^{d},\;x\mapsto W^{(2)}\sigma(W^{(1)}x+b^{(1)})+b^{(2)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{(1)}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{ff}}\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>ff</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{(1)}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{ff}}\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{(1)}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{ff}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>ff</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{(1)}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{ff}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{(2)}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d_{\mathrm{ff}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>ff</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{(2)}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d_{\mathrm{ff}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{(2)}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{(2)}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is the ReLU applied element-wise.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition A.2</span></span><span id="A1.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A transformer layer with <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> heads and width <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is a function defined as follows.</p>
<table id="A1.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Layer}:\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}\to\mathbb{R}^{n\times d},\text{Layer}(A)=A^{\prime\prime}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Layer</mtext><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>Layer</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Layer}:\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}\to\mathbb{R}^{n\times d},\text{Layer}(A)=A^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A1.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\prime}=\text{MHA}(A)+A" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>MHA</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\prime}=\text{MHA}(A)+A</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A1.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\prime\prime}=\text{FF}(A^{\prime})+A^{\prime}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>FF</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\prime\prime}=\text{FF}(A^{\prime})+A^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here <span id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">MHA</span> is the multi-head attention mechanism, and <span id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">FF</span> is a position-wise feedforward network (FFN). The terms <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+A^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+A^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are residual connections. <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\blacksquare" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">■</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\blacksquare</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Lemmas</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We start with a proof of Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 5.1. ‣ Proof. ‣ 5.1 Proof of Theorem . ‣ 5 Proofs of key results ‣ A theoretical model for task routing in mixture-of-expert transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> above.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma A.3</span></span><span id="A1.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every integer <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, there exist vectors <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1},\dots,v_{n}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1},\dots,v_{n}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> spanning a subspace of dimension <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> such that, for each <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,\dots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,\dots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th coordinate of <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the unique maximal coordinate of <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{k}=\tfrac{2\pi(k-1)}{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{k}=\tfrac{2\pi(k-1)}{n}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1,\dots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1,\dots,n</annotation></semantics></math>, and define the vectors below.</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=(\cos\theta_{1},\cos\theta_{2},\dots,\cos\theta_{n})^{\top}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=(\cos\theta_{1},\cos\theta_{2},\dots,\cos\theta_{n})^{\top},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle w</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=(\sin\theta_{1},\sin\theta_{2},\dots,\sin\theta_{n})^{\top}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=(\sin\theta_{1},\sin\theta_{2},\dots,\sin\theta_{n})^{\top}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle v_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle v_{k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex22.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\cos\theta_{k}\,u+\sin\theta_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>cos</mi><mi>θ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>sin</mi><mi>θ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\cos\theta_{k}\,u+\sin\theta_{k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(v_{k})_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(v_{k})_{j}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex23.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\cos\theta_{k}\cos\theta_{j}+\sin\theta_{k}\sin\theta_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>cos</mi><mi>θ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>cos</mi><mi>θ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>sin</mi><mi>θ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>sin</mi><mi>θ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\cos\theta_{k}\cos\theta_{j}+\sin\theta_{k}\sin\theta_{j}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\cos(\theta_{k}-\theta_{j})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\cos(\theta_{k}-\theta_{j}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Hence <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v_{k})_{k}=\cos 0=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v_{k})_{k}=\cos 0=1</annotation></semantics></math>, while for <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\neq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\neq k</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cos(\theta_{k}-\theta_{j})&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cos(\theta_{k}-\theta_{j})&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, so the maximum coordinate of <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is attained uniquely at position <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. All <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> lie in <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{span}\{u,w\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>span</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{span}\{u,w\}</annotation></semantics></math>, which has dimension <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> because <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> are linearly independent when <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. This gives the desired family of <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vectors in a <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional subspace of <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We also recall a result from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2017)</a></cite> that analyzes the memorization capacity of feedforward neural networks and shows that they can fit an arbitrary finite set of input-output vectors (see also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yun et al. (2019)</a></cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma A.4</span></span><span id="A1.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(x_{i},y_{i})\}_{i=1}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(x_{i},y_{i})\}_{i=1}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite dataset where each input <math id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and each output <math id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{q}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Assume that all inputs are distinct, i.e., <math id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}\neq x_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}\neq x_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq j</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consider a feedforward neural network <math id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> with one hidden layer of width <math id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, ReLU activations, input dimension <math id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, and output dimension <math id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then there exists a choice of parameters <math id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}(x_{i})\;=\;y_{i}\quad\text{for all }i\in[K]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}(x_{i})\;=\;y_{i}\quad\text{for all }i\in[K].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 (Sparse circuits in a single–layer transformer). ‣ 4.1 Preliminaries: knowledge circuits in transformers. ‣ 4 Theoretical results. ‣ A theoretical model for task routing in mixture-of-expert transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>.</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction of attention heads.<span id="A1.SS3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Recall that the embedding dimension is partitioned into two components: the first for template tokens and the second for dictionary tokens. We choose the query and key matrices of the first attention head so that they operate exclusively on coordinates within the first component. This yields an output <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1}(\underline{w})=v_{i}(\tau)\in\mathbb{R}^{L|\mathcal{T}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}(\underline{w})=v_{i}(\tau)\in\mathbb{R}^{L|\mathcal{T}|}</annotation></semantics></math> that depends exclusively on the underlying template <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and the current sequence index <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, we choose the query and key matrices of the second attention head so that they operate exclusively on coordinates within the second component. The output vector <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{2}(\underline{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{2}(\underline{w})</annotation></semantics></math> depends only on the dictionary tokens present in the input sequence <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{w}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Because the total number of unique template prefixes is bounded by <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L|\mathcal{T}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L|\mathcal{T}|</annotation></semantics></math>, which does not exceed the dimension of the target space <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{L|\mathcal{T}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{L|\mathcal{T}|}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can rely on a generic choice of the matrices <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{Q},W_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{Q},W_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{V}</annotation></semantics></math> to ensure the linear independence of the vectors <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{v_{i}(\tau)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v_{i}(\tau)\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Using the standard attention mechanism, each output vector <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> can be expressed as a linear combination below, where <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> are the attention weights, and <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the input embedding (comprising both the token embedding and its positional encoding) for the <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th token in the sequence.</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}(\tau)=\sum_{j=1}^{i}\alpha_{j}(x_{j}W_{V})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}(\tau)=\sum_{j=1}^{i}\alpha_{j}(x_{j}W_{V})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Because the query and key matrices only operate on the first component, the attention weights <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> are uniquely determined by the first <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> tokens in the template <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>. With the standard positional encodings, each of these prefixes yields a distinct combination of token representations and attention weights. Since there are at most <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L|\mathcal{T}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L|\mathcal{T}|</annotation></semantics></math> distinct vectors <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{v_{i}(\tau)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v_{i}(\tau)\}</annotation></semantics></math> in a space of dimension <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L|\mathcal{T}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L|\mathcal{T}|</annotation></semantics></math>, for a generic choice of weight matrices <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{Q},W_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{Q},W_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{V}</annotation></semantics></math>, the vectors will be linearly independent.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction of feedforward network.<span id="A1.SS3.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> We denote by <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(\underline{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(\underline{w})</annotation></semantics></math> the activations of the hidden layer of the feedforward network. We decompose the neurons <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> in the hidden layer into <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2|\mathcal{K}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2|\mathcal{K}|</annotation></semantics></math> blocks as follows.</span></span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I=\bigsqcup_{\underline{k}\in\mathcal{K}}I_{\underline{k}}\cup\bigsqcup_{\underline{k}\in\mathcal{K}}I^{\prime}_{\underline{k}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>I</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">∪</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi></mrow></munder><msubsup><mi>I</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I=\bigsqcup_{\underline{k}\in\mathcal{K}}I_{\underline{k}}\cup\bigsqcup_{\underline{k}\in\mathcal{K}}I^{\prime}_{\underline{k}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">Here <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|I_{\underline{k}}|=L|T(\underline{k})|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|I_{\underline{k}}|=L|T(\underline{k})|</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|I^{\prime}_{\underline{k}}|\leq N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|I^{\prime}_{\underline{k}}|\leq N</annotation></semantics></math> neurons. We choose the weights of the feedforward network such that the following properties hold.</span></p>
<ul id="A1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sparse activation (Circuit isolation):</span> By adding a large negative bias to neurons not associated with the current task, we ensure that if <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{k}^{\prime}\neq\underline{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≠</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{k}^{\prime}\neq\underline{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the ReLU activation yields <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{j}(\underline{w})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{j}(\underline{w})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all neurons <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in I_{\underline{k}^{\prime}}\cup I^{\prime}_{\underline{k}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>∪</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in I_{\underline{k}^{\prime}}\cup I^{\prime}_{\underline{k}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This isolates the computation to the sparse circuit <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(\underline{k})=I_{\underline{k}}\cup I^{\prime}_{\underline{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo>∪</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(\underline{k})=I_{\underline{k}}\cup I^{\prime}_{\underline{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Decoupled processing:</span> The block <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\underline{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\underline{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> connects exclusively to the first half of <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{\prime}(\underline{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{\prime}(\underline{w})</annotation></semantics></math> (the template representation <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1}(\underline{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}(\underline{w})</annotation></semantics></math>), while the block <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{\prime}_{\underline{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>I</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{\prime}_{\underline{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> connects exclusively to the second half (the subject representation <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{2}(\underline{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{2}(\underline{w})</annotation></semantics></math>). The final output of the network, <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z(\underline{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z(\underline{w})</annotation></semantics></math>, consists of two blocks: the first predicts the next structural word of the template (computed solely by <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\underline{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\underline{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>), and the second predicts the factual object from the dictionary (computed solely by <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{\prime}_{\underline{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>I</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{\prime}_{\underline{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">We use Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma A.4. ‣ A.2 Lemmas ‣ Appendix A Appendix: Proofs ‣ A theoretical model for task routing in mixture-of-expert transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a> above to construct the weights for these blocks. The template block <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\underline{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\underline{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>, maps the representation <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1}(\underline{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}(\underline{w})</annotation></semantics></math> to a vector representation of the next syntactic token. Because there are at most <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L|T(\underline{k})|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L|T(\underline{k})|</annotation></semantics></math> unique linearly independent prefixes, Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma A.4. ‣ A.2 Lemmas ‣ Appendix A Appendix: Proofs ‣ A theoretical model for task routing in mixture-of-expert transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a> guarantees this exact mapping can be memorized using <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|I_{\underline{k}}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|I_{\underline{k}}|</annotation></semantics></math> neurons. Concurrently, the dictionary block <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{\prime}_{\underline{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>I</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{\prime}_{\underline{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> must map the subject representations <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{2}(\underline{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{2}(\underline{w})</annotation></semantics></math> to their corresponding object representations. Since there are at most <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> entries in <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\underline{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\underline{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>, Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma A.4. ‣ A.2 Lemmas ‣ Appendix A Appendix: Proofs ‣ A theoretical model for task routing in mixture-of-expert transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a> guarantees this can be memorized using <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|I^{\prime}_{\underline{k}}|\leq N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|I^{\prime}_{\underline{k}}|\leq N</annotation></semantics></math> neurons.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction of the output layer.<span id="A1.SS3.p5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, we map the output <math id="A1.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z(\underline{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z(\underline{w})</annotation></semantics></math> of the transformer block to a probability distribution over the vocabulary <math id="A1.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. To construct the weights <math id="A1.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> of the linear unembedding layer, which maps <math id="A1.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z(\underline{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z(\underline{w})</annotation></semantics></math> to the logits <math id="A1.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>, we use Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 5.1. ‣ Proof. ‣ 5.1 Proof of Theorem . ‣ 5 Proofs of key results ‣ A theoretical model for task routing in mixture-of-expert transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>. Letting <math id="A1.SS3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=|\Sigma|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=|\Sigma|</annotation></semantics></math> denote the vocabulary size, by Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 5.1. ‣ Proof. ‣ 5.1 Proof of Theorem . ‣ 5 Proofs of key results ‣ A theoretical model for task routing in mixture-of-expert transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>, there exist <math id="A1.SS3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vectors <math id="A1.SS3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{v_{w}\}_{w\in\Sigma}\subset\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v_{w}\}_{w\in\Sigma}\subset\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> spanning a <math id="A1.SS3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional subspace, such that the <math id="A1.SS3.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>-th coordinate of <math id="A1.SS3.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> is its unique maximal coordinate. If <math id="A1.SS3.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{b_{1},b_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{b_{1},b_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is a basis for this subspace, each target vector <math id="A1.SS3.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to a valid token <math id="A1.SS3.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> can be uniquely expressed as a linear combination of these basis vectors: <math id="A1.SS3.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{w}=\alpha_{w}b_{1}+\beta_{w}b_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{w}=\alpha_{w}b_{1}+\beta_{w}b_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for some scalars <math id="A1.SS3.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{w},\beta_{w}\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{w},\beta_{w}\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">When applying Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma A.4. ‣ A.2 Lemmas ‣ Appendix A Appendix: Proofs ‣ A theoretical model for task routing in mixture-of-expert transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a> to construct the weights of the feedforward network, we choose the output vectors so that they lie within a <math id="A1.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional subspace of <math id="A1.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{L|\mathcal{T}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{L|\mathcal{T}|}</annotation></semantics></math>. The linear unembedding matrix <math id="A1.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{U}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times 2L|\mathcal{T}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{U}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times 2L|\mathcal{T}|}</annotation></semantics></math> is used to construct a mapping between these <math id="A1.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional subspaces, so that that the output is a linear combination of <math id="A1.SS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A1.SS3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in V(\underline{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in V(\underline{w})</annotation></semantics></math>. Because the <math id="A1.SS3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>-th coordinate of <math id="A1.SS3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> is uniquely maximal by Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 5.1. ‣ Proof. ‣ 5.1 Proof of Theorem . ‣ 5 Proofs of key results ‣ A theoretical model for task routing in mixture-of-expert transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>, it guarantees that the logit <math id="A1.SS3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> for the valid next token is strictly greater than the logit <math id="A1.SS3.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> for any invalid token <math id="A1.SS3.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\notin V(\underline{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\notin V(\underline{w})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">To ensure that the model has error at most <math id="A1.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, we scale the weight matrix <math id="A1.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> by a sufficiently large constant <math id="A1.SS3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Recalling that the final probability <math id="A1.SS3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(w)</annotation></semantics></math> for any token <math id="A1.SS3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> is given by the softmax function below.</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(w)=\frac{\exp(\gamma l_{w})}{\sum_{y\in\Sigma}\exp(\gamma l_{y})}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(w)=\frac{\exp(\gamma l_{w})}{\sum_{y\in\Sigma}\exp(\gamma l_{y})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS3.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">Because the logits of the valid tokens are strictly greater than those of the invalid tokens, taking the limit as <math id="A1.SS3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> forces the probability mass to concentrate entirely on the set <math id="A1.SS3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(\underline{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(\underline{w})</annotation></semantics></math> of valid next-word tokens. Therefore, for any strictly positive <math id="A1.SS3.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a scaling factor <math id="A1.SS3.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> such that the probability distribution satisfies <math id="A1.SS3.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{w\in V(\underline{w})}P(w)&gt;1-\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{w\in V(\underline{w})}P(w)&gt;1-\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. This concludes the proof of the theorem. ∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Model performance under different MoE loss formulations across varying numbers of tasks and experts. We report accuracy (%) averaged over three runs to ensure robustness.</figcaption>
<table id="A1.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A1.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">num_tasks</td>
<td id="A1.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">num_experts</td>
<td id="A1.T1.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">acc (ce loss)</td>
<td id="A1.T1.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">acc (ce + router loss)</td>
<td id="A1.T1.2.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">acc (ce + load balancing loss)</td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" rowspan="5"><span id="A1.T1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text">10</span></td>
<td id="A1.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">1</td>
<td id="A1.T1.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">88.8%</td>
<td id="A1.T1.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">88.8%</td>
<td id="A1.T1.2.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">88.8%</td></tr>
