<section id="A1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.4 </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 4.2 (Task routing in a single–layer mixture-of-experts transformer). ‣ 4.2 Main results: task routing in MoE transformers. ‣ 4 Theoretical results. ‣ A theoretical model for task routing in mixture-of-expert transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>.</h3>

<div id="A1.SS4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction of attention heads.<span id="A1.SS4.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> We follow the approach used in the proof of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 (Sparse circuits in a single–layer transformer). ‣ 4.1 Preliminaries: knowledge circuits in transformers. ‣ 4 Theoretical results. ‣ A theoretical model for task routing in mixture-of-expert transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, and choose the query, key and value matrices so that <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1}(\underline{w})=v_{i}(\tau)\in\mathbb{R}^{L|\mathcal{T}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}(\underline{w})=v_{i}(\tau)\in\mathbb{R}^{L|\mathcal{T}|}</annotation></semantics></math> is a vector that depends only on the template <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> and the index <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> (and not the subject <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>), while <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{2}(\underline{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{2}(\underline{w})</annotation></semantics></math> depends only on the subject <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Our choice of these matrices also ensures that the vectors <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{v_{i}(\tau)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v_{i}(\tau)\}</annotation></semantics></math> are linearly independent in <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{L|\mathcal{T}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{L|\mathcal{T}|}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction of the router.<span id="A1.SS4.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> We construct a linear router <math id="A1.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G:\mathbb{R}^{2L|\mathcal{T}|}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{|\mathcal{K}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G:\mathbb{R}^{2L|\mathcal{T}|}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{|\mathcal{K}|}</annotation></semantics></math> whose input is the concatenated representation <math id="A1.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{\prime}(\underline{w})=[a_{1}(\underline{w});a_{2}(\underline{w})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{\prime}(\underline{w})=[a_{1}(\underline{w});a_{2}(\underline{w})]</annotation></semantics></math>. We set the weights corresponding to the second component <math id="A1.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{2}(\underline{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{2}(\underline{w})</annotation></semantics></math> to strictly zero, so that the routing decision depends solely on the syntactic template: <math id="A1.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1}(\underline{w})=v_{i}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}(\underline{w})=v_{i}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the vectors <math id="A1.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{v_{i}(\tau)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v_{i}(\tau)\}</annotation></semantics></math> are linearly independent, we can choose the weight matrix of <math id="A1.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> that maps each <math id="A1.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> (for <math id="A1.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in T(\underline{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in T(\underline{k})</annotation></semantics></math>) exactly to the one-hot basis vector <math id="A1.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{\underline{k}}\in\mathbb{R}^{|\mathcal{K}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{\underline{k}}\in\mathbb{R}^{|\mathcal{K}|}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This ensures that all tokens generated by templates in <math id="A1.SS4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(\underline{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(\underline{k})</annotation></semantics></math> are routed to the <math id="A1.SS4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-th expert.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction of the experts.<span id="A1.SS4.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> For each <math id="A1.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{k}\in\mathcal{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{k}\in\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math>, we separate the neurons of the corresponding expert into two block components. We choose the weights such that the first and second blocks of the input <math id="A1.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z(\underline{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z(\underline{w})</annotation></semantics></math> are routed strictly through their respective expert components and mapped to the corresponding blocks of the output <math id="A1.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{\prime}(\underline{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{\prime}(\underline{w})</annotation></semantics></math>, setting all other weights to zero. We use Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma A.4. ‣ A.2 Lemmas ‣ Appendix A Appendix: Proofs ‣ A theoretical model for task routing in mixture-of-expert transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a>, about memorizing input-output vectors with a neural network with a single hidden layer, to construct the weights.</span></span></p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Example of template-based generation from WikiData5M. Given templates <math id="A1.F4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and knowledge pairs <math id="A1.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, sentences are generated by filling subject-object pairs into templates.</figcaption><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A1.F4.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="524.72" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 653.15 524.72" width="653.15"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,524.72) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#00004D;" fill="#00004D" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.49 L 0 520.23 C 0 522.71 2.01 524.72 4.49 524.72 L 648.66 524.72 C 651.14 524.72 653.15 522.71 653.15 520.23 L 653.15 4.49 C 653.15 2.01 651.14 0 648.66 0 L 4.49 0 C 2.01 0 0 2.01 0 4.49 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F7F7FF;" fill="#F7F7FF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.55 4.49 L 0.55 520.23 C 0.55 522.4 2.32 524.16 4.49 524.16 L 648.66 524.16 C 650.83 524.16 652.6 522.4 652.6 520.23 L 652.6 4.49 C 652.6 2.32 650.83 0.55 648.66 0.55 L 4.49 0.55 C 2.32 0.55 0.55 2.32 0.55 4.49 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 10.03 243.87)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:45.75em;--ltx-fo-height:19.57em;--ltx-fo-depth:16.9em;font-size:10pt;" height="504.67" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 270.82)" width="633.05"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
