</figure>
<div id="A1.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">The first block of the expert is responsible for identifying the next word in the template <math id="A1.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>. Its set of input-output pairs consists of <math id="A1.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v_{i}(\tau),e_{i}(\tau))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v_{i}(\tau),e_{i}(\tau))</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}(\tau)\in\mathbb{R}^{L|\mathcal{T}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}(\tau)\in\mathbb{R}^{L|\mathcal{T}|}</annotation></semantics></math> is the representation of the current template <math id="A1.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in T(\underline{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in T(\underline{k})</annotation></semantics></math> at position <math id="A1.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.SS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> is the embedding of the next token in the template. Since there are at most <math id="A1.SS4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> positions across <math id="A1.SS4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|T(\underline{k})|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|T(\underline{k})|</annotation></semantics></math> templates, from Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma A.4. ‣ A.2 Lemmas ‣ Appendix A Appendix: Proofs ‣ A theoretical model for task routing in mixture-of-expert transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a>, this first block requires at most <math id="A1.SS4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L|T(\underline{k})|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L|T(\underline{k})|</annotation></semantics></math> neurons to memorize these input-output pairs.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">The second block is responsible for retrieving dictionary values. We use Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma A.4. ‣ A.2 Lemmas ‣ Appendix A Appendix: Proofs ‣ A theoretical model for task routing in mixture-of-expert transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a> again, where the inputs are the subject representation from <math id="A1.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{2}(\underline{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{2}(\underline{w})</annotation></semantics></math>, and the target outputs are vector representation of the corresponding objects. Since there are at most <math id="A1.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> entries in the dictionary <math id="A1.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\underline{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\underline{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>, Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma A.4. ‣ A.2 Lemmas ‣ Appendix A Appendix: Proofs ‣ A theoretical model for task routing in mixture-of-expert transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a> guarantees this mapping can be memorized using <math id="A1.SS4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> neurons. By using separate blocks for template processing and dictionary retrieval, the total number of neurons required for the <math id="A1.SS4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-th expert is bounded by <math id="A1.SS4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L|T(\underline{k})|+N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L|T(\underline{k})|+N</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output logits and probability bound.<span id="A1.SS4.p6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Finally, we map the output <math id="A1.SS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{\prime}(\underline{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{\prime}(\underline{w})</annotation></semantics></math> of the <math id="A1.SS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-th expert to a probability distribution over the vocabulary <math id="A1.SS4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. To construct the weights <math id="A1.SS4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> of the linear unembedding layer, which maps <math id="A1.SS4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{\prime}(\underline{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{\prime}(\underline{w})</annotation></semantics></math> to the logits <math id="A1.SS4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>, we follow the approach used in the proof of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 (Sparse circuits in a single–layer transformer). ‣ 4.1 Preliminaries: knowledge circuits in transformers. ‣ 4 Theoretical results. ‣ A theoretical model for task routing in mixture-of-expert transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> above, using Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 5.1. ‣ Proof. ‣ 5.1 Proof of Theorem . ‣ 5 Proofs of key results ‣ A theoretical model for task routing in mixture-of-expert transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS4.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">When applying Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma A.4. ‣ A.2 Lemmas ‣ Appendix A Appendix: Proofs ‣ A theoretical model for task routing in mixture-of-expert transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a> to construct the experts, we choose the weights of the <math id="A1.SS4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-th expert so that their outputs lie entirely within a <math id="A1.SS4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional subspace of <math id="A1.SS4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{L|\mathcal{T}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{L|\mathcal{T}|}</annotation></semantics></math>. Using the argument from the proof of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 (Sparse circuits in a single–layer transformer). ‣ 4.1 Preliminaries: knowledge circuits in transformers. ‣ 4 Theoretical results. ‣ A theoretical model for task routing in mixture-of-expert transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> above, the linear unembedding matrix <math id="A1.SS4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{U}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times 2L|\mathcal{T}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{U}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times 2L|\mathcal{T}|}</annotation></semantics></math> yields a mapping between these <math id="A1.SS4.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional subspaces, so that the logit <math id="A1.SS4.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> for the valid next token <math id="A1.SS4.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in V(\underline{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in V(\underline{w})</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly greater than the logit <math id="A1.SS4.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> for any invalid token <math id="A1.SS4.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\notin V(\underline{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\notin V(\underline{w})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS4.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">To ensure that the model has error at most <math id="A1.SS4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, we scale the weight matrix <math id="A1.SS4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> by a sufficiently large constant <math id="A1.SS4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Using the argument from the proof of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 (Sparse circuits in a single–layer transformer). ‣ 4.1 Preliminaries: knowledge circuits in transformers. ‣ 4 Theoretical results. ‣ A theoretical model for task routing in mixture-of-expert transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> above, for any <math id="A1.SS4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a scaling factor <math id="A1.SS4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> such that the probability distribution satisfies <math id="A1.SS4.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{w\in V(\underline{w})}P(w)&gt;1-\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{w\in V(\underline{w})}P(w)&gt;1-\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. This concludes the proof of the theorem. ∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Appendix: Experiment Details</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct all experiments on PyTorch using a single NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4090 GPU with 24GB memory, with the total training time taking less than 10 hours, and report the average results over three random seeds. For data generation, we construct <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>200</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">200</annotation></semantics></math> sentences per template (see Figure <a href="#A1.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Proof. ‣ A.4 Proof of Theorem . ‣ Appendix A Appendix: Proofs ‣ A theoretical model for task routing in mixture-of-expert transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> for an illustration). For models trained with router supervision or load balancing, we set the loss weight to <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0.2</annotation></semantics></math>. The transformer backbone uses a model dimension of <math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>30</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30</annotation></semantics></math>, feedforward dimension <math id="A2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> attention heads, with a single transformer layer in all configurations. Models are trained with a batch size of <math id="A2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>64</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math> epochs using the Adam optimizer with an initial learning rate of <math id="A2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.006" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.006</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.006</annotation></semantics></math> and exponential decay factor <math id="A2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.98" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.98</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.98</annotation></semantics></math> per epoch. Full numerical results are provided in Table <a href="#A1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ A.3 Proof of Theorem . ‣ Appendix A Appendix: Proofs ‣ A theoretical model for task routing in mixture-of-expert transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</article>
